bbcode_enabled = true
bbcode_text = "[center][color=yellow]--Guide to Autotiling--[/color][/center]
Autotiling is a system that automatically
connects tiles together, reducing the 
amount of time and effort needed to create
good-looking terrain.

The two most common types of autotiling 
used are Terrain Tiling, and Brick Tiling.

First, let's cover Brick Tiling.

[center][color=yellow]--Brick Tiling--[/color][/center]
This tiling system is relatively simple.
These tiles don't usually change much based 
on their placement, and slopes are easy to understand.

Slope tiles should be placed in a diagonal line, on tiles with
empty space next to them. If a tile is above a slope tile,
you'll get a ceiling tile instead.

Next up is Terrain Tiling.

[center][color=yellow]--Terrain Tiling--[/color][/center]
In comparison to Brick Tiling, this tiling system is
somewhat complex, and uses 4 different tile types to
create terrain:


Full tiles, left slopes, right slopes, and half tiles.

Full tiles are useful for flat edges, or 
making things look blocky.

Left and right slopes are useful for making 
precise angles, combined with full tiles.

And half tiles are useful for making smooth 
natural-looking terrain, 
with round hills and bumps.

Currently, these tiles don't always connect seamlessly
with other types. This will likely be changed 
in a future update.

If you run into issues like this 
when combining tile types,
place the respective slope over the cutoff tiles.

 					-->


Now it looks seamless!


[center][color=yellow]--Boo Blocks--[/color][/center]




The boo block is a unique kind of tile, 
which turns invisible when you play
the level. These tiles will still connect terrain/brick tiles,
which makes them very useful when used in combination with 
layers.


[center][color=yellow]--Palettes--[/color][/center]
You can click the  [img=12]res://assets/icons/palette.svg[/img]  icon to change a tile's color, allowing for unique
uses of existing tiles, sometimes in ways they weren't intended for! 
This also works with objects, and backgrounds.



That should be everything you need to know about tiles. If anything above wasn't clear or you still have questions, feel free to ask in the [url=https://discord.gg/QrftDjD][color=fuchsia]Super Mario 127 Discord[/color][/url]!
"
text = "--Guide to Autotiling--
Autotiling is a system that automatically
connects tiles together, reducing the 
amount of time and effort needed to create
good-looking terrain.

The two most common types of autotiling 
used are Terrain Tiling, and Brick Tiling.

First, let's cover Brick Tiling.

--Brick Tiling--
This tiling system is relatively simple.
These tiles don't usually change much based 
on their placement, and slopes are easy to understand.

Slope tiles should be placed in a diagonal line, on tiles with
empty space next to them. If a tile is above a slope tile,
you'll get a ceiling tile instead.

Next up is Terrain Tiling.

--Terrain Tiling--
In comparison to Brick Tiling, this tiling system is
somewhat complex, and uses 4 different tile types to
create terrain:


Full tiles, left slopes, right slopes, and half tiles.

Full tiles are useful for flat edges, or 
making things look blocky.

Left and right slopes are useful for making 
precise angles, combined with full tiles.

And half tiles are useful for making smooth 
natural-looking terrain, 
with round hills and bumps.

Currently, these tiles don't always connect seamlessly
with other types. This will likely be changed 
in a future update.

If you run into issues like this 
when combining tile types,
place the respective slope over the cutoff tiles.

 					-->


Now it looks seamless!


--Boo Blocks--




The boo block is a unique kind of tile, 
which turns invisible when you play
the level. These tiles will still connect terrain/brick tiles,
which makes them very useful when used in combination with 
layers.


--Palettes--
You can click the    icon to change a tile's color, allowing for unique
uses of existing tiles, sometimes in ways they weren't intended for! 
This also works with objects, and backgrounds.



That should be everything you need to know about tiles. If anything above wasn't clear or you still have questions, feel free to ask in the Super Mario 127 Discord!
"
